  "text": "A flat piece of paper or other thin material.\n\nBelonging to or intended for all the people.\n\nFeedback stopped. Your words are still here.\n\nArrange witnessed moments on Sol’s workbench\n\nThe shaped loaves are ready for Rina’s oven.\n\nGrandma’s ropes for securing the footbridge.\n\nFree from objects that would get in the way.\n\nShows what someone intends or expects to do.\n\nWith each other, doing something as a group.\n\nWhat would have happened without the repair?\n\nThe two wooden bridge sections are separate.\n\nThe crossing stays here for everyone to use.\n\nCarrying something toward a person or place.\n\nSaid that you would definitely do something.\n\nAsk Grandma for her copy of The Empty Bench.\n\nMara tells this older story. “I” means Mara.\n\nHolding something while taking it somewhere.\n\nChange something so it works or is accurate.\n\nDuring the time that something else happens.\n\nGrandma wrote this story. “I” means Grandma.\n\nAny person, without naming a particular one.\n\nKnow what something means or why it happens.\n\nMara heard Grandma’s story at the gathering.\n\nEmphasizes something that may be surprising.\n\nA promise to keep. A garden full of stories.\n\nMara told her story to Pip, Sol and Grandma.\n\nTook hold of something suddenly and quickly.\n\nPip is carrying Mara’s page, The Torn Wing.\n\nObjects used to do a job, such as a hammer.\n\nCorresponding to the same side or position.\n\nStopped and held something that was moving.\n\nPaying attention to avoid harm or mistakes.\n\nFind out why Mara has been unable to visit.\n\nSol has brought his story to the gathering.\n\nThe child you guide through this adventure.\n\nGet back something so it can be used again.\n\nLooking at and understanding written words.\n\nOnly, exactly, or a very short time before.\n\nYour recording is sent only when you choose",
